@*
    One file, shown: what it is across the header, what it says in the body.

    The header carries the identity a reader needs before trusting the content —
    the name, where it came from, and the path it came from — and the body is the
    file, read the way that kind of file is read. The body scrolls on its own so
    the header stays put: a long file should not push the thing that tells you
    which file it is off the screen.

    Which way that is comes from the file's own name, because that is what a
    reader already knows about it. A name the highlighter has a grammar for is
    shown as code, coloured, with its lines numbered. Everything else — `.md`,
    `.txt`, a name with no extension at all — is shown as markdown, which is
    also the right answer for a file that is simply prose. Running a `.cs` file
    through the markdown parser was never a decision anyone made; it was what
    happened when markdown was the only thing this could render.

    Both shapes share one scroll region, the header above it and the name on it.
    CodeView is given Bare for exactly that reason: a block with its own frame
    and its own overflow would put a second scroll container and a second tab
    stop inside the one the reader can see.

    Both built-in shapes are read-only, and deliberately so. MarkdownView is
    given no toggle handler, so a checklist in the file renders as state rather
    than as controls, and CodeView — Bare for the reason above — brings numbered
    lines and not a text box. Neither of them writes anything back, and offering
    a checkbox that silently does nothing would be a lie.

    A host that needs a writable body supplies one. That does not make this an
    editor; it makes the writing that host's business — what the controls are,
    what a keystroke means, when it reaches disk and what is said when it does
    not. This component keeps the frame, the identity and the one scroll region,
    and stops choosing what goes inside them — it has no save state, no dirty
    flag and nothing to write with, and pretending otherwise is exactly what the
    paragraph above refuses to do.

    ---

    Three things a reader does to a file, and all three are in the header.

    Copy it, edit it, see what changed in it. They are in the header because they
    are about the file rather than about any part of it — the header is where this
    component already says which file this is, and the header is the part that
    stays on the screen while the body moves. The alternative, which is what the
    knowledge panes had, is a second bar directly under the header holding the
    same file's actions: two rows of chrome, and a reader having to learn that the
    top one names the file and the one below it operates on it.

    Read, edit and compare are one mode each, and only one at a time. Read is the
    resting state for the reason MarkdownDocument records: bodies in this product
    are read far more often than they are written. Compare is not a third body so
    much as the same body with its history beside it, and asking for it while
    typing would be asking two questions of one pane.

    None of that makes this an editor either. The Edit button chooses which body
    shows; the writable one is still the host's, still supplied, and still the
    only thing that knows what a keystroke means. Comparing is the one mode this
    component renders itself, because a comparison is a reading of two texts and
    reading is what this does — MarkdownCompare is a pure function and
    MarkdownCompareView is handed the result Bare, so the pane keeps one frame,
    one header and one scroll region, exactly as it does for code.

    What a baseline *is*, though, is the host's: see FileCompareBaseline. Reading
    a committed blob, or remembering what a file said when it was opened, is not
    something a component can do without knowing where the file came from.
*@

<article class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    <header class="file-view__header">
        <div class="file-view__identity">
            <h3 class="file-view__name">@Name</h3>
            @if (Details.Count > 0)
            {
                <p class="file-view__meta">@string.Join(" · ", Details)</p>
            }
        </div>

        <div class="file-view__aside">
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Path))
            {
                @* Truncated visually when the header is narrow, never in the DOM, so
                   a screen reader still reads the whole path. *@
                <p class="file-view__path">@Path</p>
            }

            @if (AllowCopy || OffersEdit || OffersCompare)
            {
                @* Named as a group, because "Edit" and "Compare" on their own say
                   nothing about which of several files on a page they belong to
                   (.design/accessibility.md). The file's name is what
                   distinguishes them, and it is already here. *@
                <div class="file-view__actions" role="group" aria-label="@($"{Name} actions")">
                    @if (AllowCopy)
                    {
                        @* The file, not the rendering: the source is what round
                           trips, which is the same call MarkdownView makes. *@
                        <CopyButton Text="@(Body ?? string.Empty)"
                                    Label="@($"{CopyLabel} {Name}")"
                                    ButtonCssClass="file-view__action file-view__action--copy"
                                    StatusCssClass="file-view__copy-status"
                                    ButtonTestId="@Suffix("copy")"
                                    StatusTestId="@Suffix("copy-status")" />
                    }

                    @if (OffersEdit)
                    {
                        @* A mark rather than a word, because the row it is in is
                           the header of a file and not a toolbar: the file's name
                           is what a reader is here to read. The word survives
                           whole on the label and the tooltip, which is where an
                           icon-only control has to keep it
                           (.design/accessibility.md). *@
                        <IconButton CssClass="file-view__action"
                                    AriaLabel="@EditActionLabel"
                                    Title="@EditActionLabel"
                                    OnClick="ToggleEditingAsync"
                                    TestId="@Suffix(Editing ? "done" : "edit")">@(Editing ? DoneIcon : EditIcon)</IconButton>
                    }

                    @if (OffersCompare)
                    {
                        @* A toggle and not a button whose word flips: comparing is
                           a state the pane is in, and aria-pressed is how that is
                           said without renaming the control underneath a reader.
                           Still a toggle now that the word is a glyph — the state
                           is the reason for the control, and an icon that changed
                           nothing a screen reader could hear would only be the
                           picture of one. *@
                        <ToggleButton Pressed="Comparing"
                                      PressedChanged="ToggleComparingAsync"
                                      CssClass="file-view__action"
                                      AriaLabel="@CompareLabel"
                                      Title="@CompareLabel"
                                      TestId="@Suffix("compare")"><span aria-hidden="true">@CompareIcon</span></ToggleButton>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            @if (Comparing && CompareBaselines.Count > 1)
            {
                @* Only while comparing, and only when there is a choice. A picker
                   for one baseline is a label that looks like a control, and a
                   picker beside a file nobody is comparing is a question nobody
                   asked. *@
                <div class="file-view__baselines" role="group" aria-label="@($"Compare {Name} against")">
                    @foreach (var baseline in CompareBaselines)
                    {
                        <ToggleButton BaseClass="file-view__baseline"
                                      PressedCssClass="file-view__baseline--current"
                                      Pressed="@IsSelected(baseline)"
                                      PressedChanged="() => SelectBaselineAsync(baseline)"
                                      TestId="@Suffix($"baseline-{baseline.Id}")">@baseline.Label</ToggleButton>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </header>

    @if (ShowFrontmatter && !_frontmatter.IsEmpty)
    {
        @* The file's own frontmatter, shown as the facts it is.

           Its own strip under the header rather than another row inside it. The
           header is two columns that already crowd each other when the pane is
           narrow, and a sentence describing the whole file is not part of the
           line that identifies it — the name and the path are what a reader
           checks before trusting the content, and this is the content telling
           them what it is for.

           Only when asked. A file whose first lines nobody has offered to read
           renders exactly as it did before this existed, block for block. *@
        <div class="file-view__frontmatter" data-testid="@Suffix("frontmatter")">
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_frontmatter.Description))
            {
                <p class="file-view__frontmatter-description" data-testid="@Suffix("description")">@_frontmatter.Description</p>
            }

            @if (_frontmatter.ApplyTo.Count > 0)
            {
                @* One badge per glob, and a short label in front of them: a row of
                   paths with nothing naming it reads as a breadcrumb. *@
                <div class="file-view__frontmatter-row" data-testid="@Suffix("applies-to")">
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-label">Applies to</span>
                    @foreach (var glob in _frontmatter.ApplyTo)
                    {
                        <Badge Kind="glob">@glob</Badge>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            @if (_frontmatter.Tools.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="file-view__frontmatter-row" data-testid="@Suffix("tools")">
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-label">Tools</span>
                    @foreach (var tool in _frontmatter.Tools)
                    {
                        <Badge Kind="tool">@tool</Badge>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            @* Everything else the block states, as its own key and its own words,
               in the order the file wrote them.

               The alternative is what this used to do: draw the three keys it
               recognises and take the rest out of the body along with them. A
               `name` on a skill file and a `mode` on a prompt then left the file
               without being shown anywhere, and the author of that line had no way
               to tell whether the file or the viewer had lost it. So the rule is
               the strip never hides what it does not show — which makes the
               unrecognised key a row rather than a reason to keep the noise.

               No badge and no second visual language for them: the label is the
               key as the file spells it and the value is text, in the same row the
               two lists above use. A badge says "one of a set", and a key nothing
               here recognises is not known to be one of anything. *@
            @foreach (var field in _frontmatter.Other)
            {
                <div class="file-view__frontmatter-row" data-testid="@Suffix($"field-{field.Key.ToLowerInvariant()}")">
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-label">@field.Label</span>
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-value">@field.Text</span>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @* A scroll container is an interactive element even though it is a div:
       without tabindex a keyboard user cannot reach the overflow in Chromium at
       all (.design/accessibility.md, "Non-native controls"). role + name keep it
       from being an unlabelled tab stop.

       A supplied body drops that tabindex, the way FolderView's body already
       does and for the same reason. The rule is about elements that become the
       only way to reach something: the built-in shapes render no controls at
       all, so the region is how a keyboard gets at the overflow, but a host
       supplies a body because it has controls to put in it and those are tab
       stops already. Leaving the stop in would put it in front of the editor,
       where it announces the pane and then does nothing on the way to typing. A
       host that supplies a body with nothing focusable in it and lets it
       overflow owns making that reachable — the same bargain FolderView takes.
       The role and the name are not conditional either way. *@
    <div class="file-view__body"
         role="region"
         tabindex="@BodyTabIndex"
         aria-label="@BodyLabel"
         style="@BodyStyle"
         data-testid="@BodyTestId">
        @if (ShowsComparison)
        {
            @if (SelectedBaseline?.Unavailable is { } unavailable)
            {
                <EmptyState Title="Nothing to compare against"
                            Description="@unavailable"
                            ActionCssClass="@null"
                            CssClass="file-view__empty"
                            TestId="@Suffix("compare-unavailable")" />
            }
            else
            {
                @* Bare: this pane's header already says which file it is and this
                   body already scrolls. See CodeView below — same reason, same
                   bargain. *@
                <MarkdownCompareView Section="@_comparison"
                                     Bare="true"
                                     FileName="@Name"
                                     BeforeLabel="@(SelectedBaseline?.Label ?? "Before")"
                                     AfterLabel="@AfterLabel"
                                     TestId="@Suffix("compare-view")" />
            }
        }
        else if (SuppliedBody is { } supplied)
        {
            @supplied
        }
        else if (RendersMarkdown)
        {
            @* Everything the file says about itself, drawn from the file: a
               chapter's status where its heading is, a diagram where its fence
               is, and a copy button for each chapter beside the heading that
               names it. A host that wants any of those asks for them here rather
               than parsing the same text a second time to draw them around the
               outside — which is what the knowledge panes were doing, and why a
               chapter's diagrams used to appear below the file that contained
               them. *@
            <MarkdownView Blocks="_blocks"
                          Source="@_bodyMarkdown"
                          AllowChapterCopy="@AllowChapterCopy"
                          RenderKnowledgeMetadata="@RenderKnowledgeMetadata"
                          RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields="@RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields"
                          ShowDiagramSource="@ShowDiagramSource"
                          KnowledgeFolder="@KnowledgeFolder"
                          OnKnowledgeStatusChanged="@OnKnowledgeStatusChanged"
                          OnKnowledgeNavigate="@OnKnowledgeNavigate"
                          KnowledgeHrefFor="@KnowledgeHrefFor"
                          Comments="@Comments"
                          Layout="@BodyCommentLayout"
                          OnAddComment="@OnAddComment"
                          OnResolveComment="@OnResolveComment"
                          OnEditComment="@OnEditComment" />
        }
        else
        {
            @* Numbered because this is a file and not a snippet: the reason to
               look at source in a pane like this is usually to say where in it
               something is. *@
            <CodeView Source="@Body"
                      Language="@BodyLanguage"
                      Bare="true"
                      ShowLineNumbers="true"
                      CssClass="file-view__code" />
        }
    </div>
</article>

@code {
    /// <summary>The file's name — the title, and the name of the scroll region.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The full path, shown opposite the name.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Path { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Who or what the file comes from, e.g. "GitHub Copilot".</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Source { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What kind of file it is, e.g. "Path-specific instructions".</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Kind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Size on disk. Null when the caller does not know it — better an
    /// absent fact than a fabricated zero.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public long? SizeInBytes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The file's contents, verbatim. Not read at all when
    /// <see cref="BodyContent"/> is supplied — the host is rendering the body,
    /// and it is the host that knows what the text is doing by then.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Body { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The body, rendered by the host instead of by this component: an
    /// editor, or anything else the two built-in shapes cannot be.
    /// <para>
    /// It lands inside the same scroll region, under the same header, so the
    /// pane still says which file this is and still keeps its identity on the
    /// screen while the content moves. What is inside is the host's: the
    /// name-driven choice between markdown and code is not consulted, the file
    /// is not parsed, and nothing here writes, saves or reports a change.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Omit it and this behaves exactly as it always has.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? BodyContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The writable body, shown while <see cref="Editing"/> and not before.
    /// <para>
    /// The difference from <see cref="BodyContent"/> is when it is on screen, and
    /// that difference is the whole of the Edit button: a host that supplies this
    /// gets the file rendered — status, diagrams, chapter copies, comments — until
    /// a reader asks to write, and its own editor from then on. A host that
    /// supplies <see cref="BodyContent"/> is rendering the body in both states, as
    /// it always did.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Everything about the writing is still the host's, exactly as described
    /// above. This only decides which of the two is drawn.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? EditBodyContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether to offer the file's own copy button in the header. Copies
    /// <see cref="Body"/>, so a host rendering the body itself and passing no text
    /// has nothing to copy and should leave this off.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowCopy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the copy button is called to a screen reader, before the
    /// file's name: "Copy file context-map.md".</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string CopyLabel { get; set; } = "Copy file";

    /// <summary>Whether a chapter's heading offers to copy that chapter. Passed
    /// straight to the read view, which explains what it costs to get wrong.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowChapterCopy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the header offers a way into editing at all. A file
    /// someone may only read should not show a button that refuses them — the same
    /// rule MarkdownDocument keeps.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool CanEdit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which mode the body is in. Two-way bindable, so a host that wants
    /// a file to open straight into editing can say so, and a host that does not
    /// care need not hold it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Editing { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<bool> EditingChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string EditLabel { get; set; } = "Edit";

    [Parameter]
    public string DoneLabel { get; set; } = "Done";

    /// <summary>The mark the way into editing wears. A pencil, which is what this
    /// product already draws an edit with everywhere else it draws one — a
    /// vocabulary is only a vocabulary if it is the same word each time.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string EditIcon { get; set; } = "✎";

    /// <summary>And the mark for leaving. A tick, for the same reason.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string DoneIcon { get; set; } = "✓";

    /// <summary>
    /// The versions of this file worth comparing it against, in the order a reader
    /// would reach for them. Empty — the default — offers no comparison at all.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<FileCompareBaseline> CompareBaselines { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Which baseline is being compared against. Two-way bindable
    /// because a host usually has to answer it: reading a committed version costs
    /// a process, and the moment to pay for it is when a reader picks it. Null
    /// takes the first baseline.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? CompareBaselineId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> CompareBaselineIdChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the comparison is showing. Two-way bindable for the same
    /// reason <see cref="Editing"/> is — and a host that listens can load a
    /// baseline at the moment it is asked for rather than on every render.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Comparing { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<bool> ComparingChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string CompareLabel { get; set; } = "Compare";

    /// <summary>The mark comparing wears. Two arrows facing each other, which is
    /// as close as this product's glyph vocabulary gets to a diff — nothing here
    /// already meant "these two texts, side by side". A parameter so a host with
    /// a better one is not stuck with this.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string CompareIcon { get; set; } = "⇄";

    /// <summary>What the version on screen is called in the comparison. The file
    /// as it is now, which is the reader's own copy of it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AfterLabel { get; set; } = "Now";

    /// <summary>Whether a fenced <c>meta</c> block is read as the knowledge
    /// record it is, putting each chapter's status beside its heading. Passed to
    /// the read view.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool RenderKnowledgeMetadata { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a metadata record draws the fields under the status too.
    /// Passed straight to the read view, which explains what the second tier is
    /// worth on a surface that is already showing the file.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Whether a diagram drawn from a fence keeps its own source
    /// disclosure. Passed straight to the read view and on to
    /// <c>DiagramView.ShowSource</c>. On by default, so nothing that renders a
    /// diagram today changes; off for a reading surface where a fold holding the
    /// text behind every picture is chrome the reader did not ask for. A render
    /// that failed keeps the source either way — that is DiagramView's own rule,
    /// and it has to be, because then the text is all there is left to read.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowDiagramSource { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the file's leading YAML frontmatter is drawn as a strip under the
    /// header: what the file says it is about, the globs and tools it names, and
    /// every other key it states as a plain row.
    /// <para>
    /// Off by default, like every other reading of the file's own text here. With
    /// it on, the body is given the text <em>without</em> the block, so a reader
    /// is not shown the same lines twice — once as facts and once as the divider
    /// and run-together paragraph markdown makes of them. Every key is drawn,
    /// which is what makes that removal safe: nothing leaves the body that the
    /// strip does not show. A block that states nothing is drawn as nothing and
    /// left in the body exactly as it was. A body the host supplies is never
    /// touched; see <see cref="FrontmatterSource"/>.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowFrontmatter { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Which text the frontmatter is read from. Null — the default — reads
    /// <see cref="Body"/>, which is the answer whenever this component is
    /// rendering the body itself.
    /// <para>
    /// It exists for the host that supplies <see cref="BodyContent"/>: the body is
    /// then the host's, <see cref="Body"/> is null, and the file's text is still
    /// something the host knows. Handing it in here says what the file is about
    /// without making this component responsible for what the host is drawing.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? FrontmatterSource { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which knowledge folder the file was read from, so a status can be
    /// checked against that folder's vocabulary.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public KnowledgeFolder KnowledgeFolder { get; set; } = KnowledgeFolder.Unknown;

    /// <summary>Raised when a reader picked a different status beside a chapter's
    /// heading. Nothing here writes it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeStatusChange> OnKnowledgeStatusChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated in a metadata
    /// record.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnKnowledgeNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a metadata reference to a link target.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? KnowledgeHrefFor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Remarks against the file's blocks. Empty — the default — renders
    /// the file exactly as it did before comments existed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<MarkdownComment> Comments { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Where the remarks are drawn. A file view is a reading surface
    /// wide enough for a column beside the prose, so the margin is the default
    /// here where it is not in the read view — it keeps the file reading as a file.
    /// It falls back to inline on its own below the narrow breakpoint.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MarkdownCommentLayout CommentLayout { get; set; } = MarkdownCommentLayout.Margin;

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<int> OnAddComment { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnResolveComment { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<MarkdownComment> OnEditComment { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the body is written in. Null — the default — reads it from
    /// <see cref="Name"/>, which is what a reader goes on too.
    /// <para>
    /// Set it for a file whose name cannot say: a snippet with no name, a
    /// <c>Dockerfile</c>, an extension this product spells its own way. Pass
    /// <c>markdown</c> to ask for prose back from a file whose extension would
    /// otherwise have made it code.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A language the highlighter has no grammar for still renders as code, in
    /// one colour — the same bargain CodeView makes. Losing the colour is not a
    /// reason to lose the line numbers and reflow the file as prose.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Language { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How tall the body may grow before it scrolls. Any CSS length;
    /// pass null to let the body size to its content and never scroll. Ignored
    /// when <see cref="Fill"/> is set.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? MaxHeight { get; set; } = "24rem";

    /// <summary>Take the full height the host gives this, and scroll the body
    /// inside it. For a file that lives in a pane or a column, where how tall it
    /// gets is the layout's business and not a number this component should be
    /// told.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Fill { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    // Parsed when the body changes rather than on every render: a file is long,
    // and the host re-renders for reasons that have nothing to do with it.
    private IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> _blocks = [];
    private string? _parsedBody;
    private bool _parsed;

    // The text the read view is actually given: the body, or the body without the
    // frontmatter the strip above it is showing. Held beside the blocks because
    // it is what they were parsed from — the chapter copies slice this string by
    // the offsets those blocks carry, so handing the view the other text would
    // copy the wrong lines.
    private string? _bodyMarkdown;
    private bool _parsedFrontmatterShown;
    private string? _parsedFrontmatterSource;

    // The frontmatter, read when the text it comes from changes. A file's first
    // four lines do not move because a fold opened somewhere in the pane.
    private MarkdownFrontmatter _frontmatter = MarkdownFrontmatter.None;
    private string? _readFrontmatterFrom;
    private bool _readFrontmatter;

    // The comparison, held rather than derived, for the reason the blocks below
    // are: aligning two documents by heading walks both of them, and a pane
    // re-renders every time a fold in it opens.
    private ComparedSection? _comparison;
    private string? _comparedBaseline;
    private string? _comparedBody;
    private bool _compared;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        ReadFrontmatter();
        ParseBody();
        ParseComparison();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The file's frontmatter, when a host asked for it. Nothing is read at all
    /// otherwise: a caller that does not want the strip should not pay for the
    /// reading of it, and a file that has no frontmatter is most files.
    /// </summary>
    private void ReadFrontmatter()
    {
        if (!ShowFrontmatter)
        {
            _frontmatter = MarkdownFrontmatter.None;
            _readFrontmatterFrom = null;
            _readFrontmatter = false;
            return;
        }

        var text = FrontmatterSource ?? Body;
        if (_readFrontmatter && _readFrontmatterFrom == text) return;

        _frontmatter = MarkdownFrontmatter.Read(text);
        _readFrontmatterFrom = text;
        _readFrontmatter = true;
    }

    private void ParseBody()
    {
        // A code file is never parsed as markdown. Beyond the wasted work on a
        // body that can be long, the parser would find `#` comments and `*` in
        // expressions and hand back headings and emphasis that are not there.
        //
        // Nor is a file whose body the host is rendering: the blocks would have
        // nowhere to go. That is not a micro-optimisation — a chapter open in an
        // editor re-renders on every keystroke, and parsing the file each time to
        // throw the result away is the whole file's worth of work per character.
        //
        // A file being compared is parsed all the same: the comparison is drawn
        // from the same text, and turning the comparison off has to give the file
        // back without a re-parse the reader waits for.
        if (SuppliedBody is not null || !RendersMarkdown)
        {
            _blocks = [];
            _parsedBody = null;
            _bodyMarkdown = null;
            _parsedFrontmatterSource = null;
            _parsed = false;
            return;
        }

        if (_parsed
            && _parsedBody == Body
            && _parsedFrontmatterShown == ShowFrontmatter
            && _parsedFrontmatterSource == FrontmatterSource) return;

        // The strip above is showing the frontmatter, so the body is not: read as
        // markdown those lines are a divider and a run-together paragraph of
        // `key: value`, and a reader would meet the same facts twice, the second
        // time as noise. Only the file this component is rendering itself — a
        // supplied body never reaches here, and a host's buffer is what gets
        // written back.
        //
        // The strip's own reading, not a second one, so the two cannot disagree:
        // the record hands back the text unchanged whenever it has nothing to
        // show, which is what makes "nothing is hidden that is not shown" hold
        // for every block, including one written entirely in keys the strip draws
        // as plain rows.
        //
        // A host that named a different text is the one case where they can come
        // apart, and there the body keeps everything: the strip is then describing
        // something other than what is on screen here, and lines taken out on the
        // strength of that would be hidden by a strip that never showed them.
        _bodyMarkdown = ShowFrontmatter && (FrontmatterSource is null || FrontmatterSource == Body)
            ? _frontmatter.Body
            : Body;

        // ParseDocument, not Parse: in a file a `##` is a heading, not a
        // sub-item the host will lay out elsewhere. Parse would fold everything
        // from the first `##` down into an MdSubItem that MarkdownView renders
        // nothing for, which is most of any real file.
        _blocks = MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(_bodyMarkdown);
        _parsedBody = Body;
        _parsedFrontmatterShown = ShowFrontmatter;
        _parsedFrontmatterSource = FrontmatterSource;
        _parsed = true;
    }

    /// <summary>The language in play: what the caller named, or failing that
    /// what the file is called.</summary>
    private string? BodyLanguage => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Language)
        ? CodeLanguages.ForFileName(Name)
        : Language.Trim();

    /// <summary>Markdown is the fallback rather than an entry on the language
    /// list, so it is what happens when nothing names a language at all — and
    /// naming it is how a caller asks for prose back.</summary>
    private bool RendersMarkdown =>
        BodyLanguage is null
        || BodyLanguage.Equals("md", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
        || BodyLanguage.Equals("markdown", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    private string ClassValue
    {
        get
        {
            var fill = Fill ? " file-view--fill" : string.Empty;
            var extra = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? string.Empty : " " + CssClass;
            return $"file-view{fill}{extra}";
        }
    }

    private string? BodyTestId => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-body";

    private string? Suffix(string part) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{part}";

    // Null omits the attribute entirely, which is the point: see the comment on
    // the body itself for why a host-supplied body gives up the tab stop. The
    // test is what is being drawn and not what was handed over, because a host
    // that supplies an editor still gets this component's own body while the
    // reader is comparing — and that one scrolls.
    private string? BodyTabIndex => ShowsSuppliedBody ? null : "0";

    /// <summary>
    /// The body the host is drawing, if it is the host's turn: its editor while
    /// editing, otherwise whatever it supplied for both states.
    /// <para>
    /// Comparing takes precedence over both, so this is not consulted then — a
    /// comparison is this component's own reading of the file, and a host's editor
    /// has nothing to say about the version that came before.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment? SuppliedBody =>
        ShowsComparison ? null
        : Editing && EditBodyContent is not null ? EditBodyContent
        : BodyContent;

    private bool ShowsSuppliedBody => SuppliedBody is not null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a way into editing is actually offered.
    /// <para>
    /// <see cref="CanEdit"/> is permission, and permission alone is not enough:
    /// pressing the button has to do something. It does when there is a writable
    /// body to switch to, and it does when a host is listening — a host that holds
    /// <see cref="Editing"/> can swap its own <see cref="BodyContent"/> on the way
    /// through. With neither, the button would announce an edit and then leave the
    /// same file on the screen, which is the same lie as the checkbox that writes
    /// nowhere, refused a few paragraphs up.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool OffersEdit => CanEdit && (EditBodyContent is not null || EditingChanged.HasDelegate);

    /// <summary>
    /// The layout the remarks actually get.
    /// <para>
    /// A margin is only a layout when there is something in it. A host that hands
    /// in an add handler makes every block a row whether or not anyone has said
    /// anything, and in margin mode each of those rows reserves the column — so a
    /// file nobody has remarked on would give up 16rem of its width to hold
    /// nothing, on the strength of a feature nobody has used yet. The first remark
    /// opens the column, which is also the moment it starts being worth the room.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private MarkdownCommentLayout BodyCommentLayout =>
        Comments.Count == 0 ? MarkdownCommentLayout.Inline : CommentLayout;

    /// <summary>Whether the comparison is what the body shows: asked for, and
    /// something to answer with.</summary>
    private bool ShowsComparison => Comparing && CanCompare;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a comparison can honestly be offered.
    /// <para>
    /// It needs a baseline to compare against and text to compare, and it needs
    /// the file to be prose: <see cref="MarkdownCompare"/> aligns two documents by
    /// their headings, which is what makes a diff of a chapter readable and what
    /// makes a diff of a C# file meaningless. A source file's comparison is a line
    /// diff, and this component does not have one.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool CanCompare =>
        CompareBaselines.Count > 0 && RendersMarkdown && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(Body);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a comparison is worth offering, which is a narrower question than
    /// whether one can be drawn.
    /// <para>
    /// <see cref="CanCompare"/> is the second: it decides whether the body may
    /// show a comparison, and a host that sets <see cref="Comparing"/> for a file
    /// whose baselines all match still gets a truthful — if empty — reading of it,
    /// the same bargain <see cref="CanEdit"/> and <see cref="OffersEdit"/> already
    /// keep. This one decides whether the control appears at all, and the answer
    /// is no when pressing it would show the reader the file they are already
    /// looking at.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool OffersCompare => CanCompare && HasChanges;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether any baseline has something to say that the file does not already.
    /// <para>
    /// A baseline whose text matches the body is not a change, and one the host
    /// has answered as unavailable is not a change either — it is the absence of a
    /// version to compare against, which is a reason to withhold the offer rather
    /// than to make it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A baseline still being read counts, and has to: its text is null because
    /// nothing has come back yet, not because there is nothing there. Reading a
    /// committed version costs a process and the host only pays for it when the
    /// reader asks — so a control that waited for the answer would be waiting for
    /// the press that produces it, and would never appear.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool HasChanges => CompareBaselines.Any(baseline =>
        baseline.Unavailable is null && (baseline.Text is null || !SameText(baseline.Text, Body)));

    /// <summary>Line endings are not a change. A baseline read out of git arrives
    /// with whatever the blob holds and the body with whatever the working copy
    /// does, and on this platform those routinely disagree about <c>\r</c> for a
    /// file nobody has touched.</summary>
    private static bool SameText(string? left, string? right) =>
        string.Equals(Normalized(left), Normalized(right), StringComparison.Ordinal);

    private static string Normalized(string? text) =>
        text is null ? string.Empty : text.Replace("\r\n", "\n", StringComparison.Ordinal).Replace('\r', '\n');

    /// <summary>What the one button that changes meaning is called right now. Both
    /// the accessible name and the tooltip read it, because an icon-only control
    /// has nowhere else to say which of the two it is.</summary>
    private string EditActionLabel => Editing ? DoneLabel : EditLabel;

    /// <summary>The baseline in play: the one named, or the first offered. A named
    /// baseline that is not on the list is treated as none named rather than as an
    /// error — the list is the host's and it may have just changed.</summary>
    private FileCompareBaseline? SelectedBaseline =>
        CompareBaselines.FirstOrDefault(baseline => string.Equals(baseline.Id, CompareBaselineId, StringComparison.Ordinal))
        ?? CompareBaselines.FirstOrDefault();

    private bool IsSelected(FileCompareBaseline baseline) =>
        SelectedBaseline is { } selected && string.Equals(selected.Id, baseline.Id, StringComparison.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>
    /// What the scroll region is called. The file, until it is showing two
    /// versions of the file — then it is named for the relationship, which is the
    /// wording MarkdownCompareView uses and for the reason recorded there: two
    /// regions called "domain.md" in one app leave a screen-reader user no way to
    /// tell the pane from the comparison.
    /// </summary>
    private string BodyLabel =>
        ShowsComparison ? $"{Name}, {SelectedBaseline?.Label ?? "Before"} to {AfterLabel}" : Name;

    /// <summary>Aligns the two versions, when there are two to align and either
    /// of them has changed since the last time.</summary>
    private void ParseComparison()
    {
        if (!ShowsComparison || SelectedBaseline is not { Unavailable: null } baseline)
        {
            _comparison = null;
            _comparedBaseline = null;
            _comparedBody = null;
            _compared = false;
            return;
        }

        if (_compared && _comparedBaseline == baseline.Text && _comparedBody == Body) return;

        _comparison = MarkdownCompare.Compare(baseline.Text, Body);
        _comparedBaseline = baseline.Text;
        _comparedBody = Body;
        _compared = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Into editing, or out of it — and out of comparing either way.
    /// <para>
    /// The modes are exclusive and this is where that is enforced, rather than in
    /// every host: a pane cannot be both the file being typed into and the file
    /// being read against its history. Leaving compare on and swapping the body
    /// underneath it is the version of this that looks like it works until the
    /// reader wonders which text they are editing.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ToggleEditingAsync()
    {
        Editing = !Editing;

        if (Editing && Comparing)
        {
            Comparing = false;
            await ComparingChanged.InvokeAsync(false);
        }

        await EditingChanged.InvokeAsync(Editing);
    }

    private async Task ToggleComparingAsync(bool comparing)
    {
        Comparing = comparing;

        if (Comparing && Editing)
        {
            Editing = false;
            await EditingChanged.InvokeAsync(false);
        }

        // Before the callback: a host that loads a baseline when comparing starts
        // is answering a question this component has already committed to asking.
        ParseComparison();
        await ComparingChanged.InvokeAsync(Comparing);
    }

    private async Task SelectBaselineAsync(FileCompareBaseline baseline)
    {
        CompareBaselineId = baseline.Id;
        ParseComparison();
        await CompareBaselineIdChanged.InvokeAsync(baseline.Id);
    }

    // In fill mode the flex box decides the height, so a max-height here would
    // only fight it.
    private string? BodyStyle =>
        Fill || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(MaxHeight) ? null : $"max-height: {MaxHeight}";

    /// <summary>The middle line of the header: everything known about the file
    /// that is not its name or its path, in the order a reader asks for it.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<string> Details =>
    [
        .. new[] { Source, KindValue, SizeInBytes is { } bytes ? FormatSize(bytes) : null }
            .Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part))
            .Select(part => part!)
    ];

    /// <summary>What kind of file this is. A code file that the caller did not
    /// describe says what language it is, because going Bare took away the badge
    /// CodeView would have shown — the header is the only place left that can
    /// say it. A markdown file falls back to nothing: it is the default shape,
    /// and labelling every file "Markdown" says nothing a reader did not already
    /// get from the name.</summary>
    private string? KindValue =>
        Kind ?? (RendersMarkdown ? null : CodeLanguages.Label(BodyLanguage));

    /// <summary>Bytes as the reader would say them. Formatted in the current
    /// culture, so the decimal separator is the one the rest of the app uses.</summary>
    internal static string FormatSize(long bytes)
    {
        const long Kb = 1024;
        const long Mb = Kb * 1024;
        const long Gb = Mb * 1024;

        if (bytes < 0) return string.Empty;

        return bytes switch
        {
            < Kb => $"{bytes} B",
            < Mb => $"{bytes / (double)Kb:0.#} KB",
            < Gb => $"{bytes / (double)Mb:0.#} MB",
            _ => $"{bytes / (double)Gb:0.#} GB"
        };
    }
}
